---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the concept"
  description="Amalano Glass is a pre-launch design study — what it is, what's real, and what isn't yet."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <p class="overline">About</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:var(--text-2xl);margin:0.6rem 0 1.25rem">
        A concept, kept honest
      </h1>

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          {SITE.name} is a design study — a way to explore what a small, careful
          line of stemware could look like before a single piece is made. It is
          deliberately public and deliberately unfinished.
        </p>

        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>
          The site imagines three glasses — the Universal, the Burgundy, and the
          Coupe — and the reasoning behind choosing just three. You can browse
          the shapes, read the intent, and even build a set in a local cart to
          see how it feels. It is a concept for the address
          <strong>{SITE.domain}</strong>, nothing more.
        </p>

        <h2>What's real, and what isn't</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The imagery is concept work.</strong> Every photograph is
            original, AI-generated concept photography made for this study — not
            a photograph of a finished, purchasable product.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The catalog is a draft.</strong> Names, shapes, and prices
            are merchandising hypotheses. Prices are placeholders; nothing is in
            production or in stock.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The cart is local.</strong> It lives in your browser via
            <code>localStorage</code>. It doesn't sync, doesn't track you, and
            can't send an order.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No claims we can't back.</strong> You won't find promises
            about materials, safety, care, sustainability, warranties, or
            shipping. Those require verification we don't have yet, so we don't
            make them.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Why leave it unfinished in public</h2>
        <p>
          Because the honest version is more useful than a convincing one. A
          concept that pretends to be a store would have to invent reviews,
          inventory, and guarantees. This one states plainly where it stops —
          and exactly what it would take to go further.
        </p>

        <h2>What would make it a shop</h2>
        <p>
          Finalized products with verified specifications, real inventory, a
          registered legal entity with contact details, a written
          fulfillment-and-returns policy, and a vetted payment provider. The
          <a href={href('/#preview')}>pre-launch note</a> lists the full set, and
          the <a href={href('/policies/')}>draft policies</a> describe the
          boundaries that are in force today: no sales, no data collection.
        </p>

        <p>
          Curious where it goes? For now, explore
          <a href={href('/')}>the set</a> and take the shapes for what they are —
          a considered starting point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
